---
// src/pages/irish-economy.astro
// Explainer: how Sruth and Saothar relate, and why they are two things.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const SRUTH = 'https://sruth-public.fly.dev';
const SAOTHAR = 'https://saothar-foxxelabs.fly.dev';

// Figures are quoted with the vintage they came from, because a static page
// cannot keep up with a release calendar and a number without a date is a
// number pretending not to age. The live surfaces carry the current values.
const asOf = {
  gni: '2025 vintage',
  labour: 'July 2026',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Model and the Tap | Foxxe Labs"
  description="Two ways of reading the Irish economy from CSO open data: Sruth models the circular flow between institutional sectors, Saothar taps the labour market as it is published. Why they are deliberately separate."
>
  <header class="page-header card">
    <div class="eyebrow">Foxxe Labs &middot; Irish economic data</div>
    <h1>The Model and the Tap</h1>
    <p class="sub">Two readings of the Irish economy, from the same open data, kept deliberately apart.</p>
    <p class="lede">
      Both of these read the Central Statistics Office's PxStat API &mdash; keyless, open, the same
      source data.cso.ie charts. Neither is a dashboard over an API someone else curated. The
      difference is not subject matter but <em>obligation</em>: one has to be internally consistent,
      the other has to be current. That turns out to be a hard enough distinction that they are two
      programs rather than one.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="pair">
    <article class="card proj">
      <div class="tag">The model</div>
      <h2><a href={SRUTH} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sruth</a></h2>
      <p class="gloss"><em>sruth</em> &mdash; Irish for stream, flow</p>
      <p>
        The circular flow of the Irish economy: what moves between the five institutional sectors
        &mdash; households, corporations, government, financial, rest of world &mdash; with county
        drilldowns, trade by commodity and by partner, and the energy balance underneath it.
      </p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>Cadence</dt><dd>Annual, with a quarterly cadence signal</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Shape</dt><dd>Static site over committed CSO snapshots</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Answers</dt><dd>&ldquo;Where does the money actually go?&rdquo;</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="ex">
        The number it exists to make unavoidable: on the {asOf.gni}, modified gross national income
        was <strong>&euro;334bn</strong> against a headline GDP of <strong>&euro;602bn</strong>.
        Roughly 45% of Irish GDP is not the domestic economy &mdash; it is intellectual property
        relocation, aircraft leasing and redomiciled PLCs. Every figure on the site is badged with
        whether it was measured, derived, or illustrative.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="card proj">
      <div class="tag">The tap</div>
      <h2><a href={SAOTHAR} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saothar</a></h2>
      <p class="gloss"><em>saothar</em> &mdash; Irish for labour, work, toil</p>
      <p>
        The labour market as the CSO publishes it: seasonally adjusted monthly unemployment,
        quarterly employment by economic sector, unemployment by age band and by region. Served as a
        page, as a REST API, and to AI agents over MCP.
      </p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>Cadence</dt><dd>Monthly and quarterly; polled daily at 11:05 Irish time</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Shape</dt><dd>Live service, cached, with the release date on every row</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Answers</dt><dd>&ldquo;What has just been published?&rdquo;</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="ex">
        The spread it exists to keep visible: in {asOf.labour} the unemployment rate was
        <strong>5.1%</strong> overall, <strong>4.1%</strong> for those aged 25&ndash;74, and
        <strong>12.3%</strong> for those aged 15&ndash;24. A single headline rate hides a gap of
        eight percentage points between the young and everyone else.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="card why">
    <h2 class="h">Why not one thing</h2>
    <p>
      They fail correctly in opposite directions, and that is the whole argument.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sruth is a model, so its first duty is to be consistent with itself. Its breakdowns must sum to
      their measured parents; its sectoral balances must close. Those are assertions that run before
      it builds, and if one fails the site does not ship. A model that refuses to publish rather than
      publish something that does not add up is behaving properly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saothar is a feed, so its first duty is to be current. It checks the CSO every morning after
      the 11:00 release window and serves what is there, tagged with when it was published. A feed
      that refuses to serve is simply broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wire the second into the first and you get the worst of both: a static site that now depends on
      a live service being up, and figures that arrive too late for the assertions that were the
      point. So the labour data stays where it can be current, the model stays where it can be
      consistent, and this page is the join.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card shared">
    <h2 class="h">What they do share</h2>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>
        <strong>No code is ever guessed.</strong> A PxStat table is addressed by matrix code and
        category code, and those get retired and remapped. Both projects resolve them against the
        live catalogue and re-check them on a schedule, so a code that quietly changes meaning
        becomes a failing build rather than a plausible wrong number.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aggregates are never summed with their own members.</strong> The CSO publishes
        &ldquo;Construction&rdquo; in the same list as &ldquo;Industry and Construction&rdquo; and
        &ldquo;All sectors&rdquo;. Adding up that column double-counts. Both projects work out which
        rows actually partition the total, and prove the sum rather than trusting the label.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing is quietly modelled.</strong> Neither interpolates a gap, smooths a series,
        or seasonally adjusts anything itself. Where a figure is seasonally adjusted it was adjusted
        by the CSO and says so; where a figure is computed, the formula is stated.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every number carries its date.</strong> Not the date it was fetched &mdash; the date
        the CSO published it. A revision is a visible change, not a silent one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-footer card">
    <p>
      Live: <a href={SRUTH} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sruth</a>
      &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <a href={SAOTHAR} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saothar</a>
      &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; source: <a href="https://data.cso.ie/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CSO PxStat</a>,
      Central Statistics Office, Ireland &mdash; open data.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Figures on this page are quoted with their vintage and will age; the live surfaces carry the
      current values and their release dates.
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header { margin-bottom: 18px; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .18em;
    color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .page-header h1 { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5.5vw, 3.2rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 8px; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .sub { color: var(--text); font-size: 1.1rem; font-style: italic; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch; margin: 0; }

  .pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(20rem, 1fr)); gap: 18px; margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .proj { padding: 22px; }
  .tag {
    font-size: .72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .16em;
    color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .proj h2 { font-size: 1.9rem; margin: 0 0 2px; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .proj h2 a { text-decoration: none; }
  .proj h2 a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .gloss { color: var(--muted); font-size: .85rem; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .proj p { line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 14px; }

  .facts { margin: 0 0 14px; padding: 14px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .facts > div { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; padding: 3px 0; }
  .facts dt {
    flex: 0 0 5.5rem; font-size: .72rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .12em; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 700;
  }
  .facts dd { margin: 0; font-size: .95rem; }

  .ex { color: var(--muted); font-size: .95rem; margin: 0 !important; }
  .ex strong { color: var(--text); }

  .why, .shared { padding: 22px; margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .h { font-size: .82rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .14em; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .why p { line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 14px; max-width: 76ch; }
  .why p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .rules { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .rules li {
    line-height: 1.7; padding: 12px 0 12px 16px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border); margin-bottom: 12px; max-width: 76ch;
  }
  .rules li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .rules strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 2px; }

  .page-footer { font-size: .95rem; }
  .page-footer p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }
  .fine { font-size: .82rem; margin-top: 10px !important; }
</style>
